# Critical Reflection


The design challenge was creating a programme that addresses human risks across 800 employees 
without overloading Northbridge Logistics’ security team or training budget.
A single annual presentation would be inexpensive but would not meet the needs of finance staff, warehouse workers, remote sales employees and managers. 
I therefore chose role-based activities, using existing supervisors, communication channels and departmental meetings to extend delivery. 
Another challenge was measuring behaviour change rather than treating attendance as success.
The programme combines phishing click and reporting rates, MFA coverage, data-handling assessments and incident trends, compared with a pre-launch baseline. 
These measures have limitations: for example, increased reporting may indicate greater awareness rather than more incidents.
I also treated staffing distributions and numerical targets as proposals because the brief does not provide departmental figures or baseline results.
Quarterly reviews allow the organisation to adjust activities using evidence while keeping the programme affordable and sustainable.

